will experience flooding include...\nLast Chance and Woodlin School.","ends":"2026-08-26T22:30:00-06:00","severity":"Minor","areaDesc":"Adams, CO; Arapahoe, CO; Lincoln, CO; Washington, CO","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.443caa236a55fc9790c97298cd90f89203780010.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.443caa236a55fc9790c97298cd90f89203780010.001.1"},{"event":"Flash Flood Warning","headline":"Flash Flood Warning issued August 26 at 7:08PM MDT until August 26 at 10:15PM MDT by NWS Denver CO","description":"FFWBOU\n\nThe National Weather Service in Denver has issued a\n\n* Flash Flood Warning for...\nNortheastern Elbert County in east central Colorado...\nSoutheastern Adams County in northeastern Colorado...\nCentral Arapahoe and Eastern Arapahoe County in northeastern\nColorado...\n\n* Until 1015 PM MDT.\n\n* At 708 PM MDT, Doppler radar indicated thunderstorms producing\nheavy rain across the warned area. Between 1.5 and 2.5 inches of\nrain have fallen. Additional rainfall amounts up to 1 inch are\npossible in the warned area. Flash flooding is ongoing or expected\nto begin shortly.\n\nHAZARD...Flash flooding caused by thunderstorms.\n\nSOURCE...Radar indicated.\n\nIMPACT...Flash flooding of small creeks and streams, gulches,\nroads, and poor drainage and low-lying areas.\n\n* Some locations that will experience flash flooding include...\nDeer Trail, Agate, Cottonwood Valley, Byers and Strasburg.\n\nThis includes the following streams and drainages...\nLittle Comanche Creek, Cottonwood Creek, Hay Gulch, West Bijou\nCreek, Wolf Creek, East Bijou Creek, First Creek, Comanche Creek,\nMuddy Creek, Middle Bijou Creek, Rattlesnake Creek, Badger Creek,\nKey Creek, Wilson Creek, Beaver Creek, Middlemist Creek and Agate\nCreek.","ends":"2026-08-26T22:15:00-06:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Adams, CO; Central Arapahoe, CO; Eastern Arapahoe, CO; Elbert, CO","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.30a9870d374dd2a01ff84721cde017b20c93899e.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.30a9870d374dd2a01ff84721cde017b20c93899e.001.1"},{"event":"Air Quality Alert","headline":"Air Quality Alert issued August 26 at 4:10PM MDT by NWS Denver CO","description":"The Colorado Department of Public Health and Environment has\nissued the following...\n\nWHAT...Ozone Action Day Alert.\n\nWHERE...Douglas, Jefferson, Denver, western Arapahoe, western\nAdams, Broomfield, Boulder, Larimer, and Weld Counties\n\nWHEN...400 PM Wednesday August 26 to 400 PM Thursday August 27\n\nIMPACTS...Ozone Action Day Alert is now in effect for the Front\nRange Urban Corridor.  Ozone concentrations in the Unhealthy for\nSensitive Groups category are possible both Wednesday and Thursday\nduring the afternoon and evening hours. The best chance for these\nelevated ozone levels will be in western parts of the Denver metro\narea, including the adjacent foothills.  For Colorado air quality\nconditions, forecasts, and advisories, visit:\nhttps://www.colorado.gov/airquality/colorado_summary.aspx   If\npossible, please help us reduce ozone pollution by limiting\ndriving gas and diesel-powered vehicles until at least 4 p.m.\nThursday, August 27, 2026. Colorado is also addressing ozone\npollution through state policies. For more information about\nhealth impacts and how ozone pollution forms in Colorado, visit:\nhttps://cdphe.colorado.gov/ozone-pollution-and-your-health\n\nHEALTH INFORMATION...Increasing likelihood of respiratory symptoms\nand breathing discomfort in active children and adults and people\nwith lung disease, such as asthma. Active children and adults, and\npeople with lung disease, such as asthma, should reduce prolonged\nor heavy outdoor exertion during the afternoons and evenings both\nWednesday and Thursday.","ends":"2026-08-27T16:00:00-06:00","severity":"Unknown","areaDesc":"Weld, CO; Adams, CO; Boulder, CO; Jefferson, CO; Denver, CO; Douglas, CO; Arapahoe, CO; Larimer, CO; Broomfield, CO","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.ef315fc5f2168dfb17d9e2dcb4b1708f6edc711f.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.ef315fc5f2168dfb17d9e2dcb4b1708f6edc711f.001.1"}],"afd":{"office":"BOU","issued":"2026-08-27T00:16:00+00:00","snippet":"000 FXUS65 KBOU 270016 AFDBOU Area Forecast Discussion National Weather Service Denver/Boulder CO 616 PM MDT Wed Aug 26 2026 .KEY MESSAGES... - Scattered afternoon/evening thunderstorms expected for theI-25 Corridor and plains. A few storms could become severe, with large hail and strong winds possible. - Thunderstorm coverage decreases Thursday and Friday with mainly isolated high-based showers/s","url":"https://forecast.weather.gov/product.php?site=BOU&issuedby=BOU&product=AFD&format=CI"},"hwo":null,"fwf":{"office":"BOU","issued":"2026-08-26T20:23:00+00:00","snippet":".DISCUSSION...Rest of today through Thursday...Scattered thunderstorms will move across the urban corridor and eastern plains late this afternoon and early evening with heavy rain, hail, and strong winds. Warmer and drier air moves in for Thursday with isolated high-based showers/thunderstorms. Gusty outflow winds will accompany the showers/thunderstorms while any rainfall will be light. LONG TERM","url":"https://forecast.weather.gov/product.php?site=BOU&issuedby=BOU&product=FWF&format=CI"},"coagmet":{"station_id":"den01","station_name":"Denver","distance_km":4.4,"soil_temp_5cm_f":-999,"soil_temp_15cm_f":-999,"soil_moisture_5cm":null,"soil_moisture_15cm":null,"precip_in":null,"eto_in":null,"vapor_pressure":null,"solar_radiation":0,"wind_speed_mph":1.16,"air_temp_f":66.78,"relative_humidity":0.757,"url":"https://coagmet.colostate.edu/station/den01"},"aviation":{"icao":"KBJC","distance_km":0,"raw_metar":"METAR KBJC 270335Z AUTO 13007KT 10SM CLR 21/10 A3035 RMK AO2","temp_f":70,"wind_dir":130,"wind_kt":7,"gust_kt":null,"visibility":"10+","cover":"CLR","flight_category":"VFR","altimeter":1027.9,"observed":1787801700,"raw_taf":"TAF KBJC 262340Z 2700/2724 35007KT P6SM SCT090 SCT120 TEMPO 2